task: flink-window-memory
agent: opus-flink
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/flink-window-memory
worktree: ../okay-wt-window-memory
spec: docs/benchmarks.md §20 (the second named asymmetry)

why: the last of the three things §20 named and did not build, and the
  operator asked for all of them. The section quotes no memory number
  for Flink and says why: okay advances its watermark per ELEMENT and
  evicts as it goes, while Flink's periodic generator fires every
  200 ms of WALL time — so a full-speed replay pushes eight days of
  event time through in under two seconds, the watermark advances a
  handful of times, and the engine holds panes a real deployment would
  have closed. Comparing the two profiles without saying that would be
  quoting the replay as if it were the engine.

what: `ratePerSecond` on the Flink lane (`RateLimiterStrategy` is
  already on `DataGeneratorSource`), so the same job can be run at a
  chosen event-time-to-wall-time ratio; `OkayLane.peakPanes`, which
  answers the same question EXACTLY rather than by heap sampling —
  `okay.Windows.live` knows how many panes are open. Then one test:
  okay's peak panes and peak heap, and Flink's peak heap at full speed
  and at two rates, at the quarter size §20's memory table already
  uses.

predict: Flink's peak heap falls materially as the rate falls, and
  okay's pane count does not move at all — which turns the section's
  "no memory number is quoted for that reason" into a measured
  statement with the reason attached. If Flink's heap does NOT move,
  the note in §20 is wrong and gets corrected instead.
